Orient every arc and let \(\chi_C\) be the signed incidence vector of a directed cycle \(C\), while \(\chi_{
partial S}=B^{\mathsf T}
mathbf 1_S\) is the signed incidence vector of the cut induced by \(S\subset V\), where \(B\) is the node–arc incidence matrix. Since \(B\chi_C=0\), one has \(\langle\chi_C,
chi_{
partial S}\rangle=
\langle B\chi_C,
mathbf 1_S\rangle=0\). Thus a circulation change is a combination of cycle directions, whereas a potential change modifies reduced costs by a combination of cut directions.
